            <div xml:id="d498">

                <pb facs="0849" n="1807" sameAs="frus:frus1888p2/pg1807"/>

                <head>No. 43.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Bayard to Sir L. S. Sackville
                            West</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1888-05-29"><hi rendition="#italic">May</hi> 29,
                        1888</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">Personal.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Sir Lionel</hi>: I have just sent you a reply to
                    your, notes (official) of April 30 and May 25 relating to appeals in the cases
                    of seizure in Behring Sea.</p>
                <p>I have this moment received your note of today inclosing a memorandum to the
                    effect that by reason of the failure of the proctors of the <pb facs="0850"
                        n="1808" xml:id="pg1808"/> vessels so seized, no appeals from the decrees of
                    condemnation have been entered, and the time fixed by law for doing so having
                    expired, none other than relief through diplomatic channels remains.</p>
                <p>It is much to be regretted that the order of the Attorney-General respecting
                    acceptance of security and release of these vessels had not been availed of by
                    these parties. I will, however, write at once to my colleague, the
                    Attorney-General, and see what remains to be done in his Department.</p>
                <p>Very truly, yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">T. F. Bayard.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
